    "markerStyle": "deportation",
    "explainer": "Between 1755 and 1758 British forces deported the Acadian people from their settlements — the Grand Dérangement. At Grand-Pré, Colonel Winslow’s journal preserves the roll of men and boys held in the church before the October 1755 embarkations. On Île Royale and Île Saint-Jean, the Sieur de la Roque’s 1752 census recorded every family by name and age — the same families the 1758 ships carried away, among them the Duke William, lost with over 360 aboard. A name matching these records is the beginning of a question, not proof of descent."
